2. Why Do We Need Kafka in Data Engineering?

Before Kafka, systems often communicated using direct API calls, scheduled jobs, or traditional message queues.

These approaches can still be useful, but they may not be ideal for every high-volume, real-time data pipeline.

Consider a banking application generating millions of transaction events.

The bank may need to:

Detect fraudulent transactions.

Update customer transaction histories.

Generate real-time dashboards.

Send transaction notifications.

Store data for historical analysis.


Without a shared event streaming platform, each system might need its own integration with the banking application.

This creates tight coupling and increases integration complexity.

Kafka provides a central streaming layer.

Main benefits of Kafka

Benefit	Explanation

Real-time data streaming	Events can be made available to consumers shortly after they are produced.
Scalability	Data can be distributed across multiple partitions and brokers.
Fault tolerance	Replicated partitions can help the system recover from broker failures.
Decoupling	Producers and consumers can operate independently.
Data retention	Events can be retained and replayed while they remain available.
High throughput	Kafka is designed to handle large volumes of event data.
Multiple consumers	Different applications can independently consume the same event stream.


Interview question: Why do we use Kafka?

Sample answer:

> We use Kafka when we need to move large volumes of data between systems with low latency and high throughput. It decouples data producers from consumers, supports multiple independent consumers, and provides partitioning, replication, and data retention. In data engineering, Kafka is commonly used for streaming application events, database changes, logs, and transaction data into downstream processing systems and data platforms.




