3. Batch Processing vs. Real-Time Streaming

This is one of the most important concepts for a data engineering interview.

A. Batch processing

In batch processing, data is collected over a period and processed together.

Example:

An e-commerce company generates order records throughout the day.

A scheduled ETL job runs at midnight and loads the previous day's orders into a data warehouse.

Source Database
      |
      v
  Collect Data
      |
      v
  Scheduled ETL
      |
      v
  Data Warehouse

Typical characteristics:

Scheduled processing.

Data is processed in groups.

Results may be delayed until the next job runs.

Suitable for historical reporting and large-scale transformations.


Technologies commonly used include Python, SQL, Apache Spark, and batch ETL tools.

B. Real-time stream processing

In streaming, data is processed continuously as events arrive.

Example:

A bank receives a transaction event at 10:00:01 AM.

A streaming application processes the event shortly afterward and checks whether it appears suspicious.

Transaction Application
          |
          v
         Kafka
          |
          v
   Stream Processor
          |
          v
 Fraud Detection System

Typical characteristics:

Continuous event ingestion.

Low-latency processing.

Events can be processed individually or in small groups.

Useful for monitoring, alerts, and near-real-time analytics.


Technologies include Kafka, Apache Spark Structured Streaming, and Apache Flink.

Interview comparison

Feature	Batch processing	Stream processing

Processing style	Processes accumulated data	Processes continuously arriving events
Frequency	Scheduled	Continuous or near-continuous
Latency	Often minutes to hours	Often milliseconds to seconds, depending on the system
Example	Daily sales report	Real-time transaction monitoring
Common tools	Python, SQL, Spark batch	Kafka, Spark Structured Streaming, Flink


Important: Kafka is primarily an event streaming platform, not a complete ETL transformation engine by itself.

Kafka transports and stores event streams. A separate processing system can transform, aggregate, filter, or enrich those events.

Also, Kafka is not limited to real-time systems. It can be used in architectures that combine streaming with batch processing.


