13. What Happens If a Kafka Consumer Fails?

This is another common scenario-based question.

Suppose a topic has four partitions:

Partition 0
Partition 1
Partition 2
Partition 3

Two consumers in a group process the topic.

Consumer A -> Partitions 0, 1
Consumer B -> Partitions 2, 3

Now suppose Consumer A crashes.

Kafka detects that the consumer has left the group, and the group may rebalance.

After rebalancing, Consumer B or another available consumer may receive the partitions previously assigned to Consumer A.

The new consumer resumes from the relevant committed offsets, provided the records are still available.

Important terms

Consumer failure: A consumer stops processing or leaves its group.

Rebalancing: Kafka redistributes partition assignments among group members.

Committed offset: A recorded position that the consumer group can use to resume consumption.


Interview answer

> If a consumer fails, Kafka detects its departure from the consumer group and triggers a rebalance when needed. The remaining consumers or newly joined consumers receive the affected partitions and resume from committed offsets, provided the required records remain available.




