My recommendation for your interview preparation

Since you haven't used Kafka hands-on, don't try to memorise every advanced feature at once.

Prepare in this order:

First: Understand producers, consumers, topics, partitions, offsets, brokers, and consumer groups.

Second: Practise explaining a real-time data pipeline using Kafka and Spark.

Third: Learn replication, retention, consumer lag, delivery guarantees, and CDC.

Fourth: Build the small order-event project so you can speak about actual practical exercises you've completed.

Finally: Practise scenario-based questions, especially consumer failure, duplicate events, event ordering, and increasing consumer lag.

One final interview answer to practise

If an interviewer asks, "Explain Kafka and how it is used in data engineering," you can say:

> Apache Kafka is a distributed event streaming platform used to ingest and distribute real-time data between systems. Applications publish events through producers to Kafka topics. Topics are divided into partitions to support scalability and parallel processing, and consumers read the records using offsets. Kafka can replicate partitions across brokers to improve fault tolerance.

In data engineering, Kafka is commonly used to ingest application events, database changes, and transaction data. For example, a producer can publish order events to Kafka, and Spark Structured Streaming can consume those events, transform the data, and write the results to a data lake or data warehouse.

I understand these concepts and how Kafka fits into a streaming architecture, although I haven't yet used it hands-on in a production project.



That answer covers Kafka's purpose, its architecture, a practical data engineering use case, and your experience level without overstating anything.

The goal is not just to memorise Kafka definitions. It's to understand where Kafka sits in a data pipeline, what problem it solves, and how the surrounding systems use it.